r offset-top"></div><button type=3D"button" aria-label=3D"Link" data-href=
=3D"https://magazine.sebastianraschka.com/i/200536486/1-architecture-and-mo=
del-design" class=3D"pencraft pc-reset pencraft iconButton-mq_Et5 iconButto=
nBase-dJGHgN buttonBase-GK1x3M buttonStyle-r7yGCK size_sm-G3LciD priority_s=
econdary-S63h9o" tabindex=3D"0"><svg xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" w=
idth=3D"18" height=3D"18" viewBox=3D"0 0 24 24" fill=3D"none" stroke=3D"cur=
rentColor" stroke-width=3D"2" stroke-linecap=3D"round" stroke-linejoin=3D"r=
ound" class=3D"lucide lucide-link"><path d=3D"M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a=
5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71"></path><path d=3D"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54=
l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"></path></svg></button></div></div></h2=
><p>This first section collects papers on model architecture, model-release=
 technical reports, and papers that help explain why current LLMs look the =
way they do.</p><p>One thing I find interesting about 2026 so far is that a=
rchitecture work goes beyond making transformers larger. There is a lot of =
work around</p><ul><li><p><span>hybrid architectures (for example, </span><=
em><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12374">Nemotron 3</a></em><span>, =
and </span><em><a href=3D"https://www.arxiv.org/abs/2602.17004">Arcee Trini=
ty</a></em><span>),</span></p></li><li><p><span>state space layers (</span>=
<em><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12374">Nemotron 3</a></em><span> =
and </span><em><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.15569">Mamba-3</a></em=
><span>),</span></p></li><li><p><span>MoE capacity allocation (</span><em><=
a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.21204">Scaling Embeddings Outperforms =
Scaling Experts</a></em><span>, and </span><em><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org=
/abs/2602.10604">Step 3.5 Flash</a></em><span>),</span></p></li><li><p><spa=
