2><p>The main theme this year is that long-context modeling is a main focus=
 area, both for modeling and qualitative performance.</p><p>This section co=
llects papers on sparse attention, selective state space attention, KV comp=
ression, long-context pre-training, and structured reasoning over long docu=
ment sets.</p><p><span>Even though </span><em><a href=3D"https://huggingfac=
e.co/deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Pro/blob/main/DeepSeek_V4.pdf">DeepSeek-V4: To=
wards Highly Efficient Million-Token Context Intelligence</a></em><span> co=
uld also fit into the architecture or inference-efficiency sections, I am i=
ncluding it here because its most interesting part for this overview is the=
 new attention variant, which carries over some of the sparsity aspects fro=
m the DeepSeek V3.2 (DeepSeek Sparse Attention) mechanism.</span></p><p>(Of=
 course, the report covers a lot more than attention, including a 1.6T-para=
meter MoE model, mHC residual connections, Muon optimization, post-training=
, infrastructure, and benchmark results.)</p><p>The main idea in DeepSeek V=
4 is to make sparse attention cheaper before the sparse lookup part. Earlie=
r DeepSeek models used MLA-style compressed latent cache entries, but still=
 kept one entry per token. DeepSeek V4 instead compresses the history along=
 the sequence dimension, so multiple original tokens become one compressed =
KV entry. This gives the sparse selector a much shorter history to search t=
