caption">Figure 5: MLA compresses the KV representation for each token, but=
 keeps one entry per token. CSA and HCA compress along the sequence dimensi=
on, so the sparse-attention problem gets smaller. For a longer explanation,=
 see my previous article.</figcaption></figure></div><p></p><p>CSA is the k=
ey sparse-attention variant of the two, which first compresses every small =
block of tokens into a compressed KV entry. Then it uses a DeepSeek Sparse =
Attention-style selector to choose a top-k subset of these compressed histo=
ry entries for the main attention computation. It also keeps a local slidin=
g-window branch with recent raw KV entries, which helps preserve local deta=
il.</p><p>The selector that makes the attention mask sparse is the Lightnin=
g Indexer. In simple terms, it is a lightweight attention-like scoring modu=
le that estimates which compressed history blocks are most relevant for the=
 current query. The sparse part comes from the top-k step where after scori=
ng the compressed KV entries, CSA keeps only a subset of them for the expen=
sive core attention computation instead of attending over the whole compres=
sed history. This is the same broad idea I discussed in my DeepSeek V3.2 ar=
ticle, where I explained the earlier DeepSeek sparse-attention setup in mor=
e detail: </p><div data-component-name=3D"DigestPostEmbed" class=3D"digestP=
