ing combines Chinchilla-style pre-training scaling with pass@k-style test-t=
ime scaling.</span></figcaption></figure></div><p></p><p>The main takeaway =
is that test-time scaling can make smaller, more heavily trained models com=
pute-optimal. Under standard scaling-laws, this looks like overtraining bec=
ause the model sees more tokens than the classic scaling laws would suggest=
. But if we use test-time compute scaling like self-consistency sampling (a=
ka pass@k), a smaller model can be cheaper to sample many times than to sam=
ple a larger model for a smaller number of times.</p><p>The caveat is that =
the result depends on the task and inference strategy. But for settings tha=
t rely on pass@k and other inference-/test-time scaling techniques, it is a=
