iv></h2><p>This section includes papers on training reasoning models, that =
is, reinforcement learning with verifiable reward (RLVR) for reasoning, sel=
f-distillation, policy optimization, text feedback, and multimodal chain-of=
-thought training.</p><p>The broader trend is that RLVR is now a familiar t=
echnique, so, many papers are therefore about making RL more stable, cheape=
r, more general, or easier to apply beyond narrowly verifiable tasks.</p><p=
><span>One interesting pick here is </span><em><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org=
/abs/2605.21468">You Only Need Minimal RLVR Training: Extrapolating LLMs vi=
a Rank-1 Trajectories</a></em><span>, which looks into what RLVR training a=
ctually does to the model weights, instead of only proposing another policy=
 optimization variant.</span></p><p>The main finding is that RLVR weight up=
dates appear to be much more structured than one might expect. The authors =
save checkpoints during GRPO training and look at the parameter deltas rela=
tive to the base model. Then, for each weight tensor, they stack the deltas=
 over training time and run a singular value decomposition (SVD) to identif=
y the dominant directions along which the weights move during RLVR training=
.</p><p>For those unfamiliar with SVD, in principle, SVD is related to Prin=
cipal Component Analysis, which is about finding the main directions of var=
iation in high-dimensional data. Here, the =E2=80=9Cdata=E2=80=9D are the w=
eight changes over training, so SVD helps identify the dominant directions =
in which the model parameters move during RLVR.</p><p>Surprisingly, a rank-=
1 direction per tensor preserves most of the downstream-relevant change. In=
 other words, much of the useful RLVR update seems to move along one domina=
nt direction.</p><p>They then add a second observation. Once the updates ar=
e projected onto this dominant rank-1 direction, the scalar coefficient cha=
nges almost linearly with training step. This leads to their RELEX method, =
that is, train RLVR for a short prefix, estimate the rank-1 direction, fit =
a line to the coefficient trajectory, and extrapolate a future checkpoint w=
ithout running the remaining RLVR steps. (Concretely, for each weight tenso=
r, they estimate a dominant direction (v_1) from the early RLVR checkpoints=
. Then they project each observed weight update onto that direction.)</p><d=
