>The results shown in Figure 10 are pretty clear, but it doesn=E2=80=99t me=
ntion efficiency. Sure, grep itself is cheap because it does not require em=
beddings, an index, or approximate nearest-neighbor search, etc. But if the=
 matching results are long and inserted directly into the model context, th=
e overall agent run can still become expensive because the context gets lon=
ger.</p><p>The paper kind of studies this by comparing inline result delive=
ry with a programmatic file-based setup, where search results are written t=
o disk and the agent receives only a file path or pointer. This reduces con=
text pressure, but it also adds extra tool-use steps because the model has =
to explicitly inspect the file. Again, the question is whether this has imp=
acts on efficiency.</p><p>Either way, I find the results quite convincing a=
nd relevant, and it=E2=80=99s nice to see that simpler methods perform quit=
e well. This doesn=E2=80=99t mean that vector search is dead (the paper onl=
y looks at a small slice of tasks or benchmarks), but my takeaway here is t=
hat vector search can sometimes or often be overkill (I have similar views =
about MCP).</p><ul><li><p><span>11 Jan, Dr. Zero: Self-Evolving Search Agen=
