</div></div></h2><p>I separated coding agents from the broader agent catego=
ry because this has become a substantial topic on its own.</p><p>This secti=
on includes papers on coding benchmarks, terminal capabilities, repository-=
level context files, harness optimization, and long-context coding workflow=
s.</p><p><span>One paper that definitely got my attention is </span><em><a =
href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.11988">Evaluating AGENTS.md: Are Reposit=
ory-Level Context Files Helpful for Coding Agents?</a></em><span>. It looks=
 into whether adding repository-level instruction files such as </span><cod=
e>AGENTS.md</code><span> or </span><code>CLAUDE.md</code><span> to tell cod=
ing agents how to work in a codebase is actually helpful.</span></p><p>The =
paper evaluates this in two settings. First, it uses SWE-bench Lite, where =
the authors generate context files because the original repositories do not=
 necessarily contain developer-written ones. Second, they introduce AGENTBE=
NCH, a new benchmark of 138 Python tasks from 12 repositories that already =
have developer-provided context files. The agents are then evaluated under =
three conditions: no context file, an LLM-generated context file, and, wher=
e available, a developer-written context file. The results are summarized b=
elow.</p><div class=3D"captioned-image-container"><figure><a target=3D"_bla=
