</div></div></div></a></div><p>In short, the basic idea behind diffusion la=
nguage modeling can be summarized as follows. A (non-diffusion) autoregress=
ive language model generates text from left to right. A diffusion language =
model starts from a corrupted or masked sequence and iteratively denoises i=
t. In masked diffusion, tokens are randomly replaced by mask tokens during =
training, and the model learns to recover the original clean tokens. At inf=
erence time, the model can fill in multiple positions over several denoisin=
g steps, instead of committing to one next token at a time.</p><p>One major=
 downside still is that it=E2=80=99s tricky to use them in modern agent and=
 coding harnesses due to their parallel generation nature. For instance, wh=
en we carry out and depend on tool calls, the generation is better suited a=
s a sequential setup.</p><p>Also, the tool-call shortcoming aside, the main=
 questions still are</p><ul><li><p>Can these models become competitive on q=
uality?</p></li><li><p>Can they provide meaningful speedups at similar qual=
ity?</p></li><li><p>And can they be integrated into existing LLM workflows =
without adding too much complexity?</p></li></ul><p><span>To continue with =
the theme of selecting one paper per section, one interesting paper here is=
 </span><em><a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.08676">LLaDA2.1: Speeding=
 Up Text Diffusion via Token Editing</a></em><span>. It investigates the sp=
eed aspect and asks whether a diffusion language model can use parallel dec=
oding more aggressively without locking in too many bad early choices.</spa=
n></p><p>In regular masked diffusion, the model replaces mask tokens with p=
redicted tokens. Once a mask is filled, that token is usually treated as fi=
xed. This can be brittle because if the model fills many positions in paral=
lel, one bad early token can become part of the context for the next denois=
ing step.</p><p>To address this fixed-token issue, LLaDA2.1 adds a second u=
pdate type. Besides Mask-to-Token (M2T) updates, where a masked position be=
comes a token, it also uses Token-to-Token (T2T) updates, where an already-=
filled token can be replaced by a better token later. In other words, the m=
odel can draft quickly and then revise.</p><p>The decoding rule is controll=
ed by two confidence thresholds. One threshold decides when a mask should b=
e filled. The other decides when an existing token should be edited. Loweri=
ng the mask-filling threshold makes decoding faster because more tokens are=
 generated per step. The editing threshold then gives the model a way to re=
pair some of the mistakes introduced by this more aggressive drafting. This=
 leads to (1) a Speedy Mode that drafts more aggressively and relies more o=
n edits, and (2) a Quality Mode that uses more conservative thresholds.</p>=
