 depends heavily on whether the evaluations are comprehensive, set up corre=
ctly, and ultimately measure the right thing (for example, better scores on=
 SWE-Bench don=E2=80=99t necessarily translate to better model performance =
for the end user on real-world coding tasks.)</p><p>The papers below cover =
agent evaluation, benchmark validity, adversarial attacks, long-horizon tas=
ks, structured extraction, and reasoning failures.</p><p><span>For instance=
, </span><em>AlphaEval: Evaluating Agents in Production</em><span> (</span>=
<a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.12162">https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.121=
62</a><span>) looks at agent evaluation closer to how these systems are act=
ually used. Usually, many agent benchmarks are built from clean, retrospect=
ive tasks with explicit requirements and deterministic success criteria. Ha=
ving worked in both academic research and industry, I can confirm that prod=
uction work is messier.</span></p><p>AlphaEval is a production-grounded ben=
chmark with 94 tasks sourced from seven companies across six occupational d=
omains. A key detail is that it evaluates complete agent harnesses, such as=
